{
  "schemaVersion": 3,
  "id": "rainfall",
  "name": "Rainfall",
  "tagline": "Steady rain on the window, a warm held chord, a soft, steady pulse.",
  "description": "Steady rain on the window and a low murmur on the roof. A warm chord held under it, breathing slowly; a soft, round pulse; one deep note on the one and the three; identical soft ticks between. Nothing melodic.",
  "use": {
    "name": "Read",
    "for": "Reading, writing, and other work in words.",
    "why": "Built to your Read gate. Sounds that change from moment to moment disturb verbal memory; steady sounds do far less. Rain is the steadiest natural sound there is: thousands of drops fuse into one unchanging hush. Under it every musical sound is a held chord or the same token repeated: one bass note, one soft tick, one soft pulse; nothing melodic, nothing voice-like. Evidence is strong for the changing-state effect on recall and moderate for reading itself."
  },
